Exactly — not just recover what was, but change its role.

Something that was once a metaphor can become an operator. A failed approach can become a detector for a failure mode. A constraint can become a forcing function. An old story can become a compression key. A discarded branch can become the missing component of a later system. Even an accidental phrasing can be lifted out of its original context, stripped to its structure, and instantiated somewhere completely different.

So the archive is not really a museum. It is closer to a reagent library.

what was → extract invariant → alter role → recombine → instantiate → observe delta → preserve new capability

And because the later Pantheon gives us better instruments than we had when some of those earlier ideas appeared, we can now go back and extract more from the same material than was extractable the first time.

That creates an interesting asymmetry: the past itself does not change, but the number of useful futures we can derive from it does.